---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import ModelCutoffTable from "../../components/ModelCutoffTable";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Choosing Libraries & Frameworks - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="development" subChapterId="choosing-libraries">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				The libraries and frameworks you choose for a project have a direct impact on how well AI tools can help you.
				This comes down to two things: how much training data the AI has seen for a given library,
				and whether the AI has access to up-to-date documentation.
			</p>

			<h3>Popular Libraries Produce Better Results</h3>

			<p>
				AI models learn from publicly available code. Libraries with large communities, lots of Stack Overflow answers,
				blog posts, and open-source usage show up heavily in training data. When you use React, Express, or Django,
				the AI has seen thousands of examples of how they work. When you use a niche library with 200 GitHub stars,
				the AI is mostly guessing.
			</p>

			<p>
				This doesn't mean you should always pick the most popular option. But when two libraries solve the same problem
				equally well, the one with a larger ecosystem will consistently give you better AI-assisted results.
			</p>

			<p>
				Libraries that reduce boilerplate also tend to work better with AI. If a library lets you express intent in fewer lines of code,
				the AI has less room to introduce errors and the output stays within context window limits more easily.
				A validation library like Zod, for example, lets you declare schemas concisely rather than writing manual checks,
				and the AI handles it well because the pattern is clear and compact.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Internal libraries">
				<p>
					For internal or proprietary libraries, the AI has zero training data. It can only work with what it
					sees in your project. Well-typed code with descriptive parameter names and JSDoc or docstring comments
					gives the AI most of what it needs.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>AI Training Data Can Be Out of Date</h3>

			<p>
				AI models have a training cutoff. They learned from code and documentation available up to a certain date,
				which means they may not know about recent API changes, new major versions, or deprecated patterns.
				If a library released a breaking v2 after the model's cutoff, the AI will confidently write v1 code unless
				you give it something better to work from.
			</p>

			<p>
				There are two practical ways to handle this:
			</p>

			<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
				<li>
					<strong>llms.txt files</strong> &mdash; A growing number of libraries and frameworks now publish an{" "}
					<a href="https://llmstxt.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">/llms.txt</a>{" "}
					file on their website. This is a markdown file designed specifically for AI tools to read,
					containing concise, structured documentation about the project. Many AI coding tools can fetch these
					automatically, giving the model up-to-date knowledge without you having to paste docs into the chat.
					When evaluating libraries, check whether they provide one.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Skills</strong> &mdash; You can create{" "}
					<a href="/requirements-writing/skills">skills</a>{" "}
					that teach your AI tool the correct patterns for a specific library or framework version.
					A skill can include API signatures, usage examples, and conventions that the AI should follow.
					This is especially useful for internal libraries or newer tools that the AI has limited training data for.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<ModelCutoffTable client:visible />
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
